## Exception Handling in Layered Applications

## Layer-by-Layer Strategy

```
Domain Layer     → throw typed domain errors (business violations)
Application Layer → translate domain errors to application responses
API Layer         → translate everything to HTTP responses
Infrastructure    → wrap third-party errors in domain-friendly types
```

## Domain Layer: Typed Exceptions

```typescript
// Define domain exceptions
export class OrderNotFoundError extends Error {
  constructor(orderId: string) {
    super(`Order ${orderId} not found`);
    this.name = 'OrderNotFoundError';
  }
}

export class InsufficientStockError extends Error {
  constructor(productId: string, requested: number, available: number) {
    super(`Cannot fulfil order: ${requested} requested, ${available} available`);
    this.name = 'InsufficientStockError';
  }
}

// Throw from domain logic
class Order {
  confirm(stock: number): void {
    if (stock < this.quantity) throw new InsufficientStockError(this.productId, this.quantity, stock);
    this.status = 'confirmed';
  }
}
```

## Application Layer: Translate

```typescript
async function confirmOrder(id: string): Promise<OrderDTO> {
  const order = await orderRepo.findById(id);
  if (!order) throw new OrderNotFoundError(id);

  const stock = await inventoryService.getStock(order.productId);
  order.confirm(stock); // may throw InsufficientStockError — let it propagate
  await orderRepo.save(order);
  return toOrderDTO(order);
}
```

## API Layer: Centralised Error Handler (Next.js)

```typescript
// src/lib/api-error-handler.ts
export function handleApiError(err: unknown): Response {
  if (err instanceof OrderNotFoundError) {
    return Response.json({ error: err.message }, { status: 404 });
  }
  if (err instanceof InsufficientStockError) {
    return Response.json({ error: err.message }, { status: 422 });
  }
  if (err instanceof ValidationError) {
    return Response.json({ error: err.message, fields: err.fields }, { status: 400 });
  }
  // Unknown errors — don't leak internals
  console.error('Unhandled error', err);
  return Response.json({ error: 'Internal server error' }, { status: 500 });
}

// Route handler
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  try {
    const body = await req.json();
    const result = await confirmOrder(body.orderId);
    return Response.json(result);
  } catch (err) {
    return handleApiError(err);
  }
}
```

## Infrastructure: Wrap Third-Party Errors

```typescript
class SupabaseOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
  async findById(id: string): Promise<Order | null> {
    try {
      const { data, error } = await supabase.from('orders').select().eq('id', id).single();
      if (error) throw new DatabaseError(error.message, error);
      return data ? toDomain(data) : null;
    } catch (err) {
      if (err instanceof DatabaseError) throw err;
      throw new DatabaseError('Unexpected database error', err);
    }
  }
}
```

## God Class Anti-Pattern

## What It Looks Like

```typescript
// ❌ The God class — does EVERYTHING
class ApplicationManager {
  // User management
  createUser(email: string, password: string) {}
  updateUser(id: string, data: Partial<User>) {}
  deleteUser(id: string) {}
  sendPasswordResetEmail(email: string) {}

  // Order management
  createOrder(userId: string, items: Item[]) {}
  cancelOrder(orderId: string) {}
  processPayment(orderId: string, card: CardDetails) {}
  generateInvoice(orderId: string): PDF {}

  // Product management
  addProduct(data: ProductData) {}
  updateStock(productId: string, qty: number) {}
  applyDiscount(productId: string, pct: number) {}

  // Email
  sendWelcomeEmail(user: User) {}
  sendOrderConfirmation(order: Order) {}
  sendShippingUpdate(order: Order) {}

  // Reports
  generateSalesReport(from: Date, to: Date) {}
  exportToCsv(data: unknown[]) {}

  // ... 200 more methods
}
```

## Why It's Harmful

```
❌ Untestable: to test generateInvoice() you load the whole monster class
❌ Merge conflicts: every developer touches this file constantly
❌ Fragile: change createUser() and generateSalesReport() breaks
❌ Incomprehensible: no one understands the whole class
❌ Can't reuse: can't use invoice generation without dragging in user management
```

## Refactored

```typescript
// ✅ Each class has one clear responsibility
class UserService {
  createUser(email: string, password: string) {}
