```

## YAGNI vs KISS

## YAGNI — You Aren't Gonna Need It

```
Origin: Extreme Programming (Kent Beck, Ron Jeffries)
Meaning: don't add functionality until it's actually needed

Scope → WHAT to build
```

```typescript
// ❌ YAGNI violation: building a "plugin system" nobody asked for
class NotificationService {
  private plugins: NotificationPlugin[] = []; // for future extensibility
  registerPlugin(plugin: NotificationPlugin) { this.plugins.push(plugin); }

  send(msg: string) {
    this.plugins.forEach(p => p.beforeSend?.(msg)); // nobody uses this yet
    console.log(msg);
    this.plugins.forEach(p => p.afterSend?.(msg));
  }
}

// ✅ YAGNI: build only what's needed now
function sendNotification(msg: string) {
  console.log(msg);
}
// When you need plugins, add them. Don't pre-build complexity.
```

## KISS — Keep It Simple, Stupid

```
Meaning: the code you DO write should be as simple as possible

Complexity → HOW to build it
```

```typescript
// ❌ KISS violation: unnecessary complexity
const getFullName = (user: User) =>
  [user.firstName, user.middleName, user.lastName]
    .filter(Boolean)
    .join(' ') || user.email.split('@')[0];

// ✅ KISS: simpler, and handles 99% of cases
const getFullName = (user: User) =>
  `${user.firstName} ${user.lastName}`.trim();
```

## Together

```
YAGNI → Don't build the plugin system until someone needs plugins
KISS  → When you do build it, make it as simple as possible

Both guard against over-engineering at different levels:
  YAGNI: feature/abstraction scope
  KISS:  implementation complexity
```

## The Tension

```
YAGNI says "don't abstract yet".
DRY says "don't repeat yourself".
Sometimes they conflict: the first time you repeat code, YAGNI says wait;
  the third time, extract the abstraction.
The Rule of Three: wait until you see repetition 3× before abstracting.
```

## Testing Private Methods and Fields

## The Principle: Test via the Public Interface

```typescript
// ❌ You want to test this private method:
class OrderService {
  private calculateTax(amount: number, country: string): number {
    if (country === 'GB') return amount * 0.2;
    if (country === 'US') return amount * 0.1;
    return 0;
  }

  placeOrder(items: Item[], country: string): OrderSummary {
    const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0);
    const tax = this.calculateTax(subtotal, country); // private
    return { subtotal, tax, total: subtotal + tax };
  }
}

// ✅ Test the observable behaviour via placeOrder() — tests calculateTax() implicitly
test('applies 20% tax for UK orders', () => {
  const service = new OrderService();
  const summary = service.placeOrder([{ price: 100 }], 'GB');
  expect(summary.tax).toBe(20);
  expect(summary.total).toBe(120);
});
```

## When Testing Private Methods Is a Design Smell

```typescript
// The private method is too complex to test indirectly?
// Extract it into its own class!

// ✅ Extract to a testable class
class TaxCalculator {
  calculate(amount: number, country: string): number { // now public and testable
    if (country === 'GB') return amount * 0.2;
    if (country === 'US') return amount * 0.1;
    return 0;
  }
}

// Now test TaxCalculator directly:
test('GB tax rate is 20%', () => {
  const calc = new TaxCalculator();
  expect(calc.calculate(100, 'GB')).toBe(20);
});

// OrderService uses it via injection
class OrderService {
  constructor(private taxCalc: TaxCalculator = new TaxCalculator()) {}
  // ...
}
```

## When You Absolutely Must Access Private Members

```typescript
// TypeScript: cast to any (use sparingly — it's a smell)
const result = (service as any).calculateTax(100, 'GB');

// Or use @ts-ignore for one line
// @ts-ignore
const result = service.calculateTax(100, 'GB');

// These break if you refactor the class — tightly coupled to implementation
```

## Cohesion vs Coupling

## Cohesion: Within a Module

```
High cohesion → module has ONE focused responsibility
Low cohesion  → module has MANY unrelated responsibilities

Goal: HIGH cohesion
```

```typescript
// ❌ Low cohesion: UserService does too many unrelated things
class UserService {
  createUser(data: UserData) {}
  generatePDF(order: Order) {}         // what does PDF have to do with users?
  sendMarketingEmail(campaign: Email) {} // unrelated
  calculateTax(amount: number) {}       // unrelated
}

// ✅ High cohesion: each class has one clear purpose
class UserService { createUser(data: UserData) {} }
class PdfGenerator { generatePDF(order: Order) {} }
class MarketingService { sendCampaign(email: Email) {} }
class TaxCalculator { calculate(amount: number) {} }
```

## Coupling: Between Modules

```
High coupling → change in A forces changes in B
Low coupling  → A and B interact through stable interfaces

Goal: LOW coupling
```

```typescript
// ❌ High coupling: OrderService directly instantiates PostgresDB
class OrderService {
  private db = new PostgresDB({ host: 'localhost', port: 5432 }); // tight!
  // Change DB config or switch DB → must edit OrderService
}

// ✅ Low coupling: depends on interface
interface Database { query<T>(sql: string): Promise<T[]>; }
class OrderService {
  constructor(private db: Database) {} // interface decouples from implementation
}
```

## The Relationship

```
High cohesion → smaller, focused classes → fewer reasons to depend on others → lower coupling
Low cohesion → fat classes → many things depending on them → higher coupling

"High cohesion and low coupling" is the mantra of good OO design.
```

## Types of Coupling (Low to High)

```
Data coupling    → share only simple data (function parameters)  ← best
Stamp coupling   → share complex data structures
Control coupling → one module tells another what to do (flag parameters)
Content coupling → one module reads/writes internals of another  ← worst
```

## Where Do DTOs Live?

## The Wrong Place: Domain Layer

```typescript
// ❌ DTO defined in the domain layer
export interface UserDTO { // lives in src/domain/user.dto.ts
  id: string;
  email: string;
  createdAt: string; // serialisation format — not a domain concern!
}

class UserDomainService {
  toDTO(user: User): UserDTO { // domain knows about serialisation — WRONG
    return { id: user.id, email: user.email, createdAt: user.createdAt.toISOString() };
  }
}

// Problem: the domain layer now depends on JSON format decisions
// If you change the API date format, you change the domain layer!
```

## The Right Place: Application / Interface Layer

```typescript
// ✅ Domain: pure business objects
// src/domain/user.ts
export class User {
  constructor(
    public readonly id: UserId,
    public readonly email: Email,
    public readonly createdAt: Date,
  ) {}
  isAdmin(): boolean { /* business rule */ }
}

// ✅ DTO: in application/interface layer
// src/application/users/user.dto.ts
export interface UserDTO {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  createdAt: string;
}

// ✅ Use Case (Application Service): owns the mapping
// src/application/users/get-user.use-case.ts
export class GetUserUseCase {
  constructor(private repo: UserRepository) {}

  async execute(id: string): Promise<UserDTO> {
    const user = await this.repo.findById(id);
    if (!user) throw new UserNotFoundError(id);
    return { // mapping at the boundary
      id: user.id.toString(),
      email: user.email.toString(),
      createdAt: user.createdAt.toISOString(),
    };
  }
}
```

## The Boundary Rule

```
Domain layer:            owns   → User entity (pure business)
Application layer:       owns   → DTOs + mapping logic
Interface/API layer:     passes → DTOs between HTTP and application
Infrastructure:          owns   → Persistence models + DB-to-domain mapping
```

## Caching Paginated Results with Changing Data

## The Core Problems

```
1. Offset instability:
   Page 1 (offset 0): [A, B, C, D, E]
   New item inserted: [NEW, A, B, C, D, E]
   Page 2 (offset 5): [E, F, G, H, I]  ← user misses E, sees it twice

2. Sort order changes:
   User sorts by price. Cache key: ?page=2&sort=price
   One price update → the entire sorted list changes
   Every page cache for this sort order is now invalid

3. Stale total count:
   Cached: "total: 500 items"
   10 items deleted → actual: 490
   User gets empty page 50 (offset 490)
```

## Practice 1: Cursor-Based (Keyset) Pagination

```typescript
// ❌ OFFSET pagination: unstable and hard to cache
SELECT * FROM products ORDER BY price OFFSET 40 LIMIT 10;

// ✅ Keyset pagination: stable cursor
SELECT * FROM products
  WHERE price > $lastPrice OR (price = $lastPrice AND id > $lastId)
  ORDER BY price, id
  LIMIT 10;

// Cache key: hash(sort + cursor) → stable between requests
// Same cursor = same page (doesn't shift when items are added)
```

## Practice 2: Version-Tagged Cache Keys

```typescript
// Cache the collection version (updated on writes)
async function getCollectionVersion(collection: string): Promise<string> {
  return redis.get(`version:${collection}`) ?? '0';
}

// Include version in cache key → stale pages are auto-invalidated
async function getPage(sort: string, cursor: string) {
  const version = await getCollectionVersion('products');
  const cacheKey = `products:${version}:${sort}:${cursor}`;

  const cached = await redis.get(cacheKey);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);

  const data = await db.getPage(sort, cursor);
  await redis.setEx(cacheKey, 300, JSON.stringify(data));
  return data;
}

// On any product write: increment the version → all page caches invalidated
async function onProductWrite() {
  await redis.incr('version:products');
}
```

## Practice 3: Short TTL + Stale-While-Revalidate

```typescript
// For frequently-changing data: short TTL + serve stale immediately
const PAGE_TTL = 30; // 30 seconds — accept slight staleness
const STALE_TTL = 60; // serve stale for 60s while revalidating
```

## When NOT to Cache Paginated Results

```
❌ Real-time data (live auction bids, stock prices)
❌ Personalised feeds that change per-user frequently
❌ Data with very high write rates
→ Use SSE / WebSocket streaming instead
```

## Cache Miss-Storm: Dealing with Thundering Herd

## The Problem at Scale

```
Highly trafficked product page: 10,000 requests/second
Cache TTL: 60 seconds

At t=60s: cache expires
  → All 10,000 req/s simultaneously miss
  → 10,000 parallel DB queries for the same data
  → DB CPU spikes to 100%
  → Response time degrades for ALL requests
  → Potentially cascading failure
```

## Strategy 1: Distributed Lock (Cache Stampede Prevention)

```typescript
const LOCK_TTL = 10; // max seconds to hold the lock

async function getWithLock<T>(key: string, fetcher: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {
  // Fast path: cache hit
  const cached = await redis.get(key);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);

  // Slow path: try to acquire lock
  const lockKey = `lock:${key}`;
  const lockId = crypto.randomUUID();
  const acquired = await redis.set(lockKey, lockId, { NX: true, EX: LOCK_TTL });

  if (acquired) {
    // This request won — fetch and populate cache
    try {
      const data = await fetcher();
      await redis.setEx(key, 3600, JSON.stringify(data));
      return data;
    } finally {
      // Only release if we still own the lock
      const current = await redis.get(lockKey);
      if (current === lockId) await redis.del(lockKey);
    }
  } else {
    // Lost the race — poll for the winner to populate cache
    for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
      await sleep(100);
      const result = await redis.get(key);
      if (result) return JSON.parse(result);
    }
    // Fallback: fetch directly (lock timed out)
    return fetcher();
  }
}
```

## Strategy 2: Stale-While-Revalidate + Background Refresh

```typescript
interface CacheEntry<T> {
  data: T;
  expiresAt: number;  // when to start refreshing
  serveUntil: number; // when to refuse to serve stale data
}

async function getStaleWhileRevalidate<T>(
  key: string,
  fetcher: () => Promise<T>,
  ttl: number = 60,
  staleTtl: number = 120
): Promise<T> {
  const raw = await redis.get(key);

  if (raw) {
    const entry: CacheEntry<T> = JSON.parse(raw);
    const now = Date.now();

    if (now < entry.expiresAt) {
      return entry.data; // fresh — serve immediately
    }

    if (now < entry.serveUntil) {
      // Stale but within grace period
      // Trigger background refresh if no lock held
      const lockKey = `refresh:${key}`;
      const locked = await redis.set(lockKey, '1', { NX: true, EX: ttl });
      if (locked) {
        setImmediate(async () => {
          const fresh = await fetcher();
          const newEntry: CacheEntry<T> = {
            data: fresh,
            expiresAt: Date.now() + ttl * 1000,
            serveUntil: Date.now() + staleTtl * 1000,
          };
          await redis.setEx(key, staleTtl, JSON.stringify(newEntry));
        });
      }
      return entry.data; // serve stale immediately — no wait!
    }
  }

  // Cache miss or too stale — must fetch
  const data = await fetcher();
  const entry: CacheEntry<T> = {
    data,
    expiresAt: Date.now() + ttl * 1000,
    serveUntil: Date.now() + staleTtl * 1000,
  };
  await redis.setEx(key, staleTtl, JSON.stringify(entry));
  return data;
}
```

## Strategy 3: L1 (In-Process) + L2 (Redis)

```typescript
// In-process LRU cache as L1 — prevents Redis storms during refill
const l1 = new LRUCache<string, unknown>({ max: 500, ttl: 5_000 }); // 5s TTL

async function get<T>(key: string, fetcher: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {
  // L1: in-process (sub-ms)
  const l1hit = l1.get(key);
  if (l1hit) return l1hit as T;

  // L2: Redis
  const l2hit = await redis.get(key);
  if (l2hit) {
    const data = JSON.parse(l2hit) as T;
    l1.set(key, data); // populate L1
    return data;
  }

  // Miss: fetch and populate both levels
  const data = await getWithLock(key, fetcher);
  l1.set(key, data);
  return data;
}
// During cache refill: L1 serves thousands of requests per second
// with zero Redis calls, eliminating the stampede at the Redis level
```
